{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Deskpad — Mobile / native UI{% endblock %}
{% block hint %}SwiftUI &amp; Compose, from Twig{% endblock %}

{#
   A native screen, authored in a template.

   `native(type, options, children)` is the whole authoring surface — one generic
   function rather than 37 element-specific ones, because the type is data and the
   template language should not have to know 37 names.

   Upstream's Blade equivalent is a tag precompiler that rewrites `<native:*>` tags into
   collector calls, specifically to *bypass* Blade's component lifecycle for speed. Twig
   needs none of that: its functions are already calls, so there is nothing to compile
   around.

   `native_publish()` sends the frame. Off a device there is nothing to send it to, so the
   publisher captures the tree and returns it — which is the only reason a native screen can
   be looked at in a browser at all. It takes an optional callback registry; a template used
   as a whole screen wants the default, and a component lifecycle re-rendering on an
   interaction has to pass its own, because the id arriving from the device only resolves in
   the registry that minted it.
#}
{% set tree = native_publish(
    native('column', {class: card}, [
        native('text', {text: 'Ada Lovelace', fontSize: 22, fontWeight: 'bold'}),
        native('text', {text: 'ada@example.com', fontSize: 14, color: '#64748b'}),

        native('divider'),

        native('row', {layout: {gap: 12}}, [
            native('column', {class: muted}, [
                native('text', {text: 'Notes', fontSize: 12, color: '#64748b'}),
                native('text', {text: '12', fontSize: 20, fontWeight: 'bold'}),
            ]),
            native('column', {class: muted}, [
                native('text', {text: 'Synced', fontSize: 12, color: '#64748b'}),
                native('text', {text: '2 min ago', fontSize: 20, fontWeight: 'bold'}),
            ]),
        ]),

        native('toggle', {value: true, label: 'Sync over cellular', onChange: 'toggleSync'}),

        native('spacer'),

        native('button', {label: 'Sign out', variant: 'destructive', onPress: 'signOut'}),
    ])
) %}

{% block body %}
    <h1>Native UI, authored in Twig</h1>
    <p class="lede">
        No HTML and no WebView: the tree below is the wire format the SwiftUI and Jetpack
        Compose renderers consume directly. The bundle's <code>UiWireFormatTest</code>
        byte-compares trees like this one against upstream's own collector, so the shape
        is verified even though the device is not.
    </p>

    <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
            <h2>The template</h2>
            <pre>{% verbatim %}{% set tree = native_publish(
    native('column', {class: 'flex-1 p-4 gap-3 bg-white rounded-xl'}, [
        native('text', {text: 'Ada Lovelace', fontSize: 22, fontWeight: 'bold'}),
        native('text', {text: 'ada@example.com', fontSize: 14, color: '#64748b'}),
        native('divider'),
        native('row', {layout: {gap: 12}}, [ … ]),
        native('toggle', {value: true, label: 'Sync over cellular', onChange: 'toggleSync'}),
        native('spacer'),
        native('button', {label: 'Sign out', variant: 'destructive', onPress: 'signOut'}),
    ])
) %}{% endverbatim %}</pre>
            <p class="dim" style="font-size:.8rem;margin:.6rem 0 0">
                Styling uses the same Tailwind subset as upstream, through the
                <code>class</code> option — <em>not</em> by parsing in the controller and
                passing the result as <code>layout</code>. The parser emits a camelCase
                intermediate vocabulary and splits across three wire buckets, so
                <code>{layout: parsed}</code> publishes <code>flexGrow</code> where the
                renderer wants <code>flex_grow</code> and puts <code>bg</code> in the layout
                bucket instead of the style one: accepted, published, silently ignored.
                <code>class</code> routes through <code>StyleApplier</code>, which is the
                piece that knows where each key belongs. The parser itself reproduces
                upstream's output byte-for-byte across 684 tokens; the only intentional
                divergence is a bug fix.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>What goes on the wire</h2>
            <pre>{{ tree|json_encode(constant('JSON_PRETTY_PRINT') b-or constant('JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES')) }}</pre>
            <p class="dim" style="font-size:.8rem;margin:.6rem 0 0">
                <code>onPress</code> handlers are gone from the payload: each became a
                numeric callback id, derived by hashing the expression within its scope.
                The device sends the id back and it is used for exactly one thing — a
                lookup. Nothing from the device ever becomes a method name.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
